NXR-201|Fixed-frame wire schema|HIL wire protocol|The HIL codec shall encode the documented 512-byte frame using explicit little-endian fields and zero padding.|The wire proposal exists but no frozen executable codec currently establishes compatibility.|Golden frames match byte for byte on host and target; native struct packing changes do not alter encoding.|Cross-check independent encoders and a published offset table including all boundary fields.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|512 decoded bytes: 40 header, 468 payload region and 4 CRC bytes.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|The wire proposal exists but no frozen executable codec currently establishes compatibility.
NXR-202|CRC coverage controls|HIL wire protocol|The codec shall validate CRC-32C over all first 508 decoded bytes including padding before dispatch.|Validating only meaningful payload would leave framing metadata and padding unchecked.|Single-bit changes in header, payload and padding fail; the standard check string yields E3069283.|Use independent bitwise and optimized implementations plus fixed known-answer frames.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Four-byte little-endian CRC; polynomial 82F63B78 with declared initial and final XOR.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|Validating only meaningful payload would leave framing metadata and padding unchecked.
NXR-203|COBS resynchronization bounds|HIL wire protocol|The decoder shall bound encoded accumulation and recover at a delimiter after malformed or oversized input.|USB and serial read boundaries do not correspond to logical frame boundaries.|All-zero payload and maximal nonzero runs round-trip; oversized or truncated frames never grow storage or dispatch commands.|Feed arbitrary read fragments, consecutive delimiters and corrupted code bytes followed by a valid frame.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|At most 516 encoded bytes including delimiter; statically reserved 576-byte slots.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|USB and serial read boundaries do not correspond to logical frame boundaries.
NXR-204|Packet rejection ordering|HIL wire protocol|Packet validation shall reject magic, version, reserved fields, length and CRC errors before command side effects.|A checksum pass is insufficient to authorize a structurally invalid command.|Each malformed field independently causes rejection and leaves run state, output ownership and command cache unchanged.|Compare state snapshots around one-field mutations and wrong-endian fixtures.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Constant frame ceiling; no payload-dependent allocation or callback before validation.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|A checksum pass is insufficient to authorize a structurally invalid command.
NXR-205|Session and boot identity binding|HIL wire protocol|Every state-changing command shall match the negotiated session nonce and current boot identity.|Sequence numbers alone cannot isolate sessions across reboot and USB enumeration.|A captured pre-reset command and a command from another host session cannot start or stop work.|Replay frames across reconnection and controlled resets with unchanged command numbers.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|128-bit session nonce and 64-bit boot identity per wire proposal; entropy quality stated separately.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|Sequence numbers alone cannot isolate sessions across reboot and USB enumeration.
NXR-206|Command retry idempotency|HIL wire protocol|The target shall return a cached response for an exact retry of the last command without repeating side effects.|Transport retries need execution identity rather than blind re-dispatch of commands.|Duplicate START and STOP execute once; reuse of the same command ID with different bytes is rejected.|Drop the first response, resend identical and modified requests and count actual state transitions.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One command in flight; one bounded request-response cache.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|Transport retries need execution identity rather than blind re-dispatch of commands.
NXR-207|Transport sequence interpretation|HIL wire protocol|The receiver shall distinguish transport loss and duplicate frames from logical report or command identity.|Transport sequencing and logical event deduplication solve different problems.|A repeated fault report with a new transport sequence is not a new fault; missing transport sequence increments loss evidence.|Inject reordered, duplicate and wrapping transport sequences with stable logical identities.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Fixed tracking window and documented sequence wrap comparison.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|Transport sequencing and logical event deduplication solve different problems.
NXR-208|Status paging coherence|HIL wire protocol|Paged status shall carry a sample epoch and page bounds so a host never merges incompatible snapshots.|A single 512-byte packet cannot hold an unbounded worker inventory.|More than eight workers are reported over pages; a changed epoch during collection invalidates the aggregate.|Mutate worker counters between pages and test missing, duplicate and out-of-range pages.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Eight 32-byte thread slots per status frame; bounded page count declared by profile.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|A single 512-byte packet cannot hold an unbounded worker inventory.
NXR-209|Capability validity encoding|HIL wire protocol|Optional telemetry fields shall carry explicit validity and capability metadata rather than treating zero as unsupported.|Zero measurements can otherwise create false claims about unavailable hardware features.|A supported zero-error ECC counter differs from absent ECC hardware; unsupported latency does not display as zero latency.|Round-trip absent, valid-zero, saturated and stale metrics in schema fixtures.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Validity bits and typed units fit the fixed schema; no implicit feature inference.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|Zero measurements can otherwise create false claims about unavailable hardware features.
NXR-210|Protocol compatibility negotiation|HIL wire protocol|Handshake shall reject incompatible major schemas and negotiate only documented compatible capabilities before CONFIGURE.|Version numbers need an explicit compatibility rule before mixed host and firmware builds are safe.|Unknown mandatory features and incompatible packet layout fail without arming workers; optional unknown fields follow the stated policy.|Run old/new schema fixture pairs and unsupported-command probes.|NXR-137|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Bounded capability bitmap and finite handshake deadline.|docs/hardware-in-loop.rst,docs/hil-verification-plan.rst|Version numbers need an explicit compatibility rule before mixed host and firmware builds are safe.
